<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fürth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fürth is a city in Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, adjoining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. Long a market town with one of the largest Jewish communities in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, it grew into an industrial city in the nineteenth century. In December 1835 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-railway">Ludwig Railway</a> between Nuremberg and Fürth, the first German railway worked by steam, opened between the two cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/furth.jpg" alt="Fürth" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamran Salimi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuerther_Innenstadt_Mrz_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fürth lies in the Middle Franconia region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Its centre is only seven km from that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, and it forms a continuous conurbation with Nuremberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a> and Schwabach. The historic centre stands to the east and south of the Rednitz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pegnitz">Pegnitz</a> rivers, which join to form the Regnitz northwest of the Old Town. To the north lies the fertile market-gardening area called the Knoblauchsland, the garlic country. The name comes from the German word for ford, because the first settlements grew up around one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Founded as a Franconian settlement in the mid-8th century, Fürth is first mentioned in a document of 1 November 1007, in which Emperor Heinrich II gave his property there to the newly created Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. Market privileges were granted but later lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> under Heinrich III, and restored from 1062, by which time Nuremberg was already the more important town. In 1524, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, Fürth became a Protestant town like Nuremberg, though ties with Bamberg meant there were always some Catholics.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> the town lost about half its population. When Croatian soldiers set fire to it in 1634 it burned for several days and was almost completely destroyed, and at the end of the war only 800 people lived there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> settled in 1685, and by 1700 restoration was complete and the population had risen to about 6,000. Until the end of the 18th century a representative of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> administered it. Fürth passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1806 and from 1818 was a city responsible for its own administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are mentioned in Fürth as early as 1440. In 1528 the Margrave of Ansbach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-the-pious">George the Pious</a>, allowed two Jews, Perman and Uriel, to settle in return for high taxes, and the community grew. By the 17th century there was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmudic-academy">Talmudic academy</a> of considerable repute, a synagogue was built in 1617, and in 1653 the first Jewish hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was founded. The position of Jews in Fürth earned it the name Franconian Jerusalem, a phrase based on an older reference meant as an insult. The Jewish Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, opened in 1999, occupies a house that goes back to the 17th century.</p><p>Fürth survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> with less damage than most German cities and has many historic buildings. The Protestant Church of St Michael, dating from about 1100, is the oldest, and the Rathaus, built in 1840–50, has a tower modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-vecchio">Palazzo Vecchio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. The toy industry remains a major employer, and the football club SpVgg Fürth has won the German championship three times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fürth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Railways spread rapidly after the line from Nuremberg to <b>Fürth</b> opened in 1835 and the Leipzig–Dresden line followed in 1839, and the Rhineland, the Ruhr, Saxony and Silesia began to industrialise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany's first railway, the Ludwig Railway between Nuremberg and <b>Fürth</b>, opened in December 1835, and the Ludwig Canal linking the Main and the Danube was completed in 1846.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
